::GEO_U06_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>Which measurement does surface area represent? {
=A. The total area covering the outside of a solid#Surface area measures the exterior faces or curved surfaces.
~B. The space inside a solid#That is volume.
~C. The distance around one face#That is perimeter of a face.
~D. The height of the solid#Height is one linear dimension.
}

::GEO_U06_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>A cube has side length 4. What is its surface area? {
~A. 16 square units#This is the area of one face.
=B. 96 square units#A cube has 6 faces, each 4&sup2;=16, so 6(16)=96.
~C. 64 cubic units#That is volume.
~D. 24 units#This adds edge lengths, not face areas.
}

::GEO_U06_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>Which part of 2&pi;r&sup2; + 2&pi;rh represents the two bases of a cylinder? {
~A. 2&pi;rh#This represents the curved side area.
~B. &pi;rh#This is not the two-base term.
=C. 2&pi;r&sup2;#Each base has area &pi;r&sup2;, so two bases are 2&pi;r&sup2;.
~D. &pi;r&sup2;h#This is volume, not base area.
}

::GEO_U06_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>Which unit is appropriate for surface area of a cone? {
~A. cm#Centimeters are linear units.
~B. cm&sup3;#Cubic centimeters measure volume.
~C. degrees#Degrees measure angles.
=D. cm&sup2;#Surface area is measured in square units.
}

::GEO_U06_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>A prism net is made of rectangles. How can the net help find surface area? {
=A. Find the area of each face and add the areas#A net shows every outside face of the solid.
~B. Multiply all edge lengths#Multiplying all edge lengths does not find surface area.
~C. Find only the largest rectangle#Surface area includes all faces, not only one.
~D. Count the vertices#Vertices do not determine surface area.
}

